window.WIKI_PAGES = [{"id":"README.md","title":"Agent-Era Software Engineering Wiki","md":"# Agent-Era Software Engineering Wiki\n\n> **Distilled from Lauren Tan (`@poteto`) — SpaceXSI / Grok Bot, React Core Team (React Compiler), ex-Cursor, ex-Meta, ex-Netflix**\n> Drawn from [*\"All Code Is Slop (Except Mine)\"*](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O_zcc6Xl12g) (AI Engineer CODE 2026), her live deep-dive with Matt Pocock, and the 24 software engineering principles encoded in [`pstack/`](../../pstack).\n\n---\n\n## What This Wiki Is About\n\nThis is **not** a plugin manual or slash-command reference. It is a **Software Engineering Wiki** exploring a single, urgent question:\n\n> **When AI agents make writing code nearly free, what separates a codebase that compounds in quality from one that drowns in slop?**\n\nLauren Tan's answer is counterintuitive: **it is almost never about prompting tricks.** Shipping ~3,000 pull requests a month individually (and ~11,000/month across her team with a ~0.5% revert rate) did not come from cleverer system prompts. It came from **six months of investing in the engineering environment around agents**—treating the codebase, type system, static analyzer, verification harness, and feedback loops as a high-trust system where doing the right thing is the path of least resistance.\n\nHer motto sums it up: **\"If you want to go fast, go deep first.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Wiki Architecture\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    HOME[\"00. Wiki Home<br/>Mental Models & Reading Paths\"]\n    P1[\"01. The Inverse Pyramid of Trust<br/>(Core Framework & Escalation Ladder)\"]\n    P2[\"02. Codebase as a Garden<br/>(Layer 1: Architecture & Local Context)\"]\n    P3[\"03. Types, Boundaries & Domain Modeling<br/>(Layer 1 → 2: Correctness by Construction)\"]\n    P4[\"04. Static Analysis & Why Comments Lie<br/>(Layer 2: Lint, Ratchets & Invariants)\"]\n    P5[\"05. Verification as the New Code Review<br/>(Layer 3: Runtime, Formal & Quantitative Proof)\"]\n    P6[\"06. Debugging, Design & Scientific Craft<br/>(Root Causes, Prototypes & Verifiable Units)\"]\n    P7[\"07. From Line Cook to Head Chef<br/>(Layers 4–5: Delegation, Intent & Factories)\"]\n    P8[\"08. Applying the Pyramid to Go & Other Languages<br/>(internal/, golangci-lint, arch_test, -race, fuzz)\"]\n\n    HOME --> P1\n    P1 --> P2\n    P1 --> P3\n    P1 --> P4\n    P1 --> P5\n    P1 --> P6\n    P1 --> P7\n    P1 --> P8\n```\n\n---\n\n## Articles\n\n| # | Article | Core Theme | Key Case Studies & Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **01** | [**The Inverse Pyramid of Trust**](./01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md) | Why deterministic constraints beat `AGENTS.md` prose; the cost gradient from architecture down to human review. | Low-Trust vs. High-Trust loop; the \"Meat Proxy\" Chrome DevTools story; solving the same bug at all 5 pyramid levels; Verifiability × Reversibility matrix. |\n| **02** | [**Codebase as a Garden & Local-Context Architecture**](./02-codebase-as-a-garden.md) | Why your repository is the agent's largest prompt, and how directory structure and ruthless deletion steer output. | Grok Bot's eight 10k-line god files → Dune; the `setTimeout` weed (1 → 183 timers); banning `useEffect` in product code; Reader Load's 30-second test. |\n| **03** | [**Types, Boundaries & Domain Modeling**](./03-types-boundaries-and-domain-modeling.md) | Making illegal states unconstructable, trusting internal types, and eliminating shared mutable state. | Discriminated unions vs. boolean bags; constructive modeling (`NonEmpty<T>`, `{ start, durationMs }`); Simplest Total Type; schema-derived types; idempotency & worktree separation. |\n| **04** | [**Static Analysis, Agent-Directed Lint & Why Comments Lie**](./04-static-analysis-and-anti-comment-hygiene.md) | Turning repeated human review comments into mechanical walls that tell agents the exact fix. | Cutting 65,824 → 1,227 comments; writing agent-directed lint errors; the Ratchet Pattern (runnable example); time-boxed exceptions; observe-only background sweepers. |\n| **05** | [**Verification as the New Code Review**](./05-verification-as-code-review.md) | Spending ~70% of tokens proving real behavior and shifting human review from diffs to evidence artifacts. | Defeating tautological tests (2-edit litmus test); Feature Maps & ARIA-driven control CLIs; 7-question benchmark vetting; TLA+, Lean, Bombadil & visual statecharts. |\n| **06** | [**Debugging, Design & Scientific Engineering**](./06-debugging-design-and-scientific-thinking.md) | How top engineers diagnose failures, escape retry loops, and settle design decisions empirically. | Root cause vs. symptom guard; *Attack the Premise*; throwaway prototypes vs. abstract plans; *Sequence into Verifiable Units*; scientific hillclimbing. |\n| **07** | [**From Line Cook to Head Chef: Delegation & Factories**](./07-human-in-the-loop-delegation-and-factories.md) | Scaling from one agent to parallel/overnight loops without multiplying unchecked slop. | Michelin-starred kitchen metaphor; transmission of intent; author ≠ verifier; 4 readiness gates before automation; solo multi-harness playbook. |\n| **08** | [**Applying the Pyramid to Go (& Other Languages)**](./08-golang-and-cross-language-guide.md) | Translating Electron/TS ideas to Go (`internal/`, `golangci-lint`, `arch_test.go`, `-race`, fuzzing, `benchstat`) + Python/Rust Rosetta Stone. | Clearing up the \"no lint in Go\" myth; complete `.golangci.yml` with `depguard`/`forbidigo`/`nolintlint`; zero-dependency Go boundary test. |\n\n---\n\n## Suggested Reading Paths\n\n- **Start here (The Big Picture):** Read [**01. The Inverse Pyramid of Trust**](./01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md) → [**02. Codebase as a Garden**](./02-codebase-as-a-garden.md).\n- **Go developer fast track:** Read [**01. Inverse Pyramid**](./01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md) → [**08. Applying the Pyramid to Go**](./08-golang-and-cross-language-guide.md) → [**05. Verification**](./05-verification-as-code-review.md).\n- **\"My agents keep making the same mistakes over and over\":** Read [**02. Codebase as a Garden**](./02-codebase-as-a-garden.md) → [**03. Types & Boundaries**](./03-types-boundaries-and-domain-modeling.md) → [**04. Static Analysis & Comments**](./04-static-analysis-and-anti-comment-hygiene.md).\n- **\"Tests pass, builds are green, yet broken code still ships\":** Read [**05. Verification as Code Review**](./05-verification-as-code-review.md) → [**06. Debugging & Scientific Craft**](./06-debugging-design-and-scientific-thinking.md).\n- **\"I want to run parallel or overnight agents safely as a solo dev\":** Read [**01. Inverse Pyramid**](./01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md) → [**05. Verification**](./05-verification-as-code-review.md) → [**07. Line Cook to Head Chef**](./07-human-in-the-loop-delegation-and-factories.md).\n\n---\n\n*Tip: Open [`index.html`](./index.html) directly in any browser for a small, offline static wiki reader. For the `pstack` plugin catalog appendix, see [`docs/pstack/`](../pstack/README.md).*\n"},{"id":"01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md","title":"01. The Inverse Pyramid of Trust","md":"# 01. The Inverse Pyramid of Trust\n\n> **Core Thesis:** *\"The meta-job of software engineering is no longer writing code faster—it is figuring out how to maintain or improve code quality while massively increasing output. That is not about blind trust in agents; it is about investing in the agent's environment until you have objective reasons to trust what comes out.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Productivity Inversion: Why Old Habits Break\n\nIn 2024, Lauren Tan walked into work and reviewed roughly **3 pull requests a day** written by human colleagues. By late 2026, that number was closer to **300 pull requests a day** generated by fleets of coding agents.\n\nBefore coding agents, software engineering operated under one binding constraint: **human typing and thinking throughput**. Only rare outliers (\"coding machines\" like Bret Taylor rewriting Google Maps in a weekend) could out-produce a team. Code review existed as a human safety net because human output was slow enough for another human to read every line.\n\nAI agents collapsed that constraint overnight. Suddenly, **anyone can produce thousands of lines of code a day**. When production volume increases 100×, the bottleneck moves ruthlessly to two new places:\n\n1. **Verifiable Trust** — knowing whether a change actually works and obeys system invariants without reading every line by hand.\n2. **Environment Quality** — ensuring the codebase itself guides agents toward good patterns rather than multiplying bad ones.\n\n### The Low-Trust Loop vs. The High-Trust Loop\n\nMost engineers adopting coding agents get trapped in what Lauren calls the **Low-Trust Loop**:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph LOW[\"Low-Trust Loop (The Slop Trap)\"]\n        direction LR\n        L1[\"Agent writes code\"] --> L2[\"Code contains slop / subtle bugs\"]\n        L2 --> L3[\"Human reads diff & tests manually\"]\n        L3 --> L4[\"Human corrects agent in chat\"]\n        L4 --> L1\n    end\n\n    subgraph HIGH[\"High-Trust Loop (Self-Correcting Environment)\"]\n        direction LR\n        H1[\"Agent writes code inside clean boundaries\"] --> H2[\"Compiler / lint / static checks reject bad shapes\"]\n        H2 --> H3[\"Agent runs real app & captures proof artifacts\"]\n        H3 --> H4[\"Independent verifier checks diff & behavior\"]\n        H4 --> H5[\"Human reviews evidence & architecture\"]\n        H5 -. \"Repeat mistake? Promote up the Pyramid\" .-> H2\n    end\n```\n\nIn the Low-Trust Loop, **you become a human go-between**—or, in Lauren's memorable phrase from optimizing Cursor's Agent pane, **\"the meat proxy between my agent and Chrome DevTools.\"** She realized she was launching the app herself, reading flame graphs and heap snapshots, typing her observations into chat, waiting for the agent to edit code, and then re-running the profiler herself.\n\nAs long as you are the eyes, hands, and memory of the loop, **running 5 parallel agents does not make you 5× faster—it gives you 5× as many interruptions waiting for your attention.**\n\n---\n\n## 2. The Inverse Pyramid of Trust\n\nWhen an agent makes a mistake for the third time, almost every developer has the same reflex: open `AGENTS.md` (or `CLAUDE.md` or `.cursor/rules`) and add one more warning sentence—*\"NEVER import the database client inside an API route!\"*\n\nLauren Tan argues this instinct is completely upside down. **Prose is the weakest enforcement mechanism in software engineering.** Instead, she models where to invest engineering effort as an **Inverse Pyramid of Trust**:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    L1[\"LAYER 1: CODEBASE & ARCHITECTURE (Widest · Deterministic · Lowest Marginal Cost)<br/>API design, directory boundaries, data shapes, framework primitives, deleting legacy code\"]\n    L2[\"LAYER 2: STATIC ANALYSIS & TESTS (Deterministic · Automatic Rejection)<br/>Strict types, compilers, dependency-graph lint, agent-directed lint errors, behavior tests, CI\"]\n    L3[\"LAYER 3: VERIFICATION — RUNTIME & FORMAL (High-Signal Evidence)<br/>Driving the real app (CDP/Playwright/CLI), Feature Maps, TLA+ model checking, Lean proofs, UI fuzzing\"]\n    L4[\"LAYER 4: PROSE (Narrow · Non-Deterministic · Judgment Calls Only)<br/>Skills, playbooks, AGENTS.md, coding style docs\"]\n    L5[\"LAYER 5: HUMAN REVIEW (Narrowest · Highest Cost · Finite Bandwidth)<br/>Reviewing proof artifacts, statecharts, decision logs, product direction, irreversible actions\"]\n\n    L1 --> L2 --> L3 --> L4 --> L5\n```\n\n### Why the Pyramid Is Inverted\n\nTwo forces run in opposite directions as you move down the pyramid:\n\n| Property | Top (Layers 1–2: Code & Static Checks) | Middle (Layer 3: Verification) | Bottom (Layers 4–5: Prose & Human Review) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Determinism** | **100% deterministic.** Gives the exact same answer on every run, every model, every harness. | **Empirical / Formal.** Proves actual behavior or mathematical invariants. | **Probabilistic.** An agent can ignore a prose rule when context fills up; humans get tired. |\n| **Feedback Speed** | **Milliseconds to seconds.** Agent hits a wall and self-corrects inside the same turn. | **Seconds to minutes.** Catches behavioral, visual, and concurrency bugs before merge. | **Minutes to hours.** Blocks asynchronously on human attention. |\n| **Marginal Cost per PR** | **Near zero** once built. Scales effortlessly from 1 PR/day to 1,000 PRs/day. | **Token + compute cost** (~70% of daily tokens), completely scalable with machines. | **Human attention** — strictly capped and non-scalable. |\n| **Cross-Harness Portability** | **100% portable** across Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex, and human contributors. | **100% portable** when wrapped in repo scripts/CLIs. | **Tool-specific** and prone to drifting across harnesses. |\n\n> **The Golden Rule of Corrections (`Encode Lessons in Structure`):**\n> *Before adding another line to `AGENTS.md`, ask whether a lint rule or test can reject the mistake mechanically. Before adding a lint rule, ask whether a better API, type, or directory design can make the mistake impossible to write in the first place.*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Concrete Walkthrough: Solving the Same Bug at All 5 Layers\n\nTo see the Inverse Pyramid in action, take a classic recurring bug in backend web/API projects: **an agent adds a new admin endpoint and forgets to enforce tenant/organization authorization, querying the database directly by `recordId`.**\n\nWatch what happens when you handle that repeat mistake at each layer of the pyramid:\n\n### Level 5 — Human Review (Most Expensive, Never Compounds)\nYou spot the missing tenant check during code review and leave a comment: *\"Please scope this query by `orgId`!\"*\n- **Outcome:** Fixed in *this* PR. Next Tuesday, a fresh agent writes another route and makes the exact same mistake.\n\n### Level 4 — Prose in `AGENTS.md` (Fragile, Unenforceable)\nYou add a bold warning to `AGENTS.md`:\n> *\"CRITICAL: Always filter every database query by `ctx.orgId`!\"*\n- **Outcome:** Works 70% of the time. When a chat gets long or an agent opens a file that doesn't show the pattern, it forgets. Nothing fails when the rule is skipped.\n\n### Level 3 — Behavioral / Runtime Verification (High Signal, Catches Before Merge)\nYou write a cross-tenant integration test / verification script that creates `Org A` and `Org B`, attempts to fetch `Org A`'s record with `Org B`'s token, and asserts HTTP `403`/`404`.\n- **Outcome:** Catches the leak if the endpoint is wired into the test suite—but a brand-new endpoint might still ship untested if the agent forgets to add it to the suite.\n\n### Level 2 — Static Analysis & Lint (Automatic, Instant Feedback)\nYou add a dependency-graph lint rule forbidding `src/routes/**` from importing `src/io/rawDb`—with an **agent-directed error message**:\n```text\nerror: Routes cannot import 'src/io/rawDb' directly.\nUse a tenant-scoped repository from 'src/io/repos/' (see 'src/io/repos/projects.ts')\nwhich requires a verified TenantContext.\n```\n- **Outcome:** The instant any agent tries to query the DB directly from a route, the linter rejects it and points straight to the right module. The agent fixes itself in 5 seconds.\n\n### Level 1 — Codebase Architecture & Types (Impossible to Write Wrong)\nYou redesign the data-access API so `rawDb` is unexported/private to `src/io/tenantDb.ts`, and the **only** exported function requires an authenticated `TenantContext` branded at the auth middleware boundary:\n\n```ts\n// Only auth middleware can construct a verified TenantContext\nexport type TenantContext = { readonly orgId: OrgId; readonly __brand: \"TenantContext\" };\n\n// Every query helper automatically injects WHERE org_id = ctx.orgId\nexport function findProject(ctx: TenantContext, id: ProjectId): Promise<Project | null> {\n  return rawDb.projects.findFirst({ where: { id, orgId: ctx.orgId } });\n}\n```\n\n- **Outcome:** It is now **physically impossible** for any agent (or human) to call `findProject` without a verified `TenantContext`. You never review for missing tenant filters again—and you can **delete** the warning from `AGENTS.md`!\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Autonomy Boundary: Verifiability × Reversibility\n\nA natural question—pressed directly by Matt Pocock in his interview with Lauren Tan—is: *\"How far can you push this? Is autonomous merging safe for finance, healthcare, or destructive database migrations?\"*\n\nLauren's answer separates two orthogonal axes that many developers conflate:\n\n1. **Verifiability:** Can a machine programmatically check whether the change achieved its goal without regressions?\n2. **Reversibility (\"Two-Way Door\" vs. \"One-Way Door\"):** If a subtle mistake slips through anyway, can you revert it cleanly in 60 seconds, or is data/money/trust permanently damaged?\n\n```mermaid\nquadrantChart\n    title Autonomy Readiness Matrix\n    x-axis Low Verifiability --> High Verifiability\n    y-axis Irreversible --> Reversible\n    quadrant-1 High Autonomy Safe\n    quadrant-2 Human Judgment Needed\n    quadrant-3 Always Human-Led\n    quadrant-4 Verify + Human Merge Gate\n    UI fixes & refactors: [0.85, 0.85]\n    CLI features & perf: [0.80, 0.72]\n    UX taste & layout: [0.28, 0.78]\n    Product strategy: [0.20, 0.22]\n    DB migrations: [0.76, 0.22]\n    Billing & auth: [0.82, 0.14]\n```\n\nNotice the critical insight in Quadrant 4: **a passing test suite proves assertions passed; it will not recover dropped production rows.** That is why even in `pstack`'s own autonomy rules (*Never Block on the Human*), irreversible actions—deploys, data deletion, force-pushes to shared branches, external customer messages—**always** require explicit human confirmation.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Honest Reading of Lauren Tan's Reported Outcomes\n\nIn *\"All Code Is Slop (Except Mine)\"*, Lauren shared her team's real production metrics on Grok Bot (an Electron desktop app) across five months:\n\n- **PR volume:** Rose from 809/month (June) to **10,926/month** (October) across 227 contributors (~3,000+/month personally). She explicitly notes in interviews that a huge fraction of those PRs are **gardening** (restructuring, deleting dead patterns, hardening guardrails), *not* 3,000 net-new user features.\n- **Revert rate:** Held steady around **~0.5%** on a relative basis even as volume grew 13×.\n- **User-facing latency (May → Nov):** Cold launch −39% (`2,304 → 1,395 ms`), long-chat median typing latency −65% (`8.4 → 2.9 ms`), chat switching −40% to −44%.\n- **Honest regressions shown on the same slide:** Idle memory rose +12% (`488 → 545 MiB`), window resize latency rose +130% (`3.0 → 6.9 ms`), and startup JS/CSS bundle grew +285% (`2.0 → 7.7 MB`).\n\n**The transferable takeaway is not the raw PR count—it is the order of operations:**\n1. Clean the codebase agents read from (Layer 1).\n2. Make recurring corrections executable via types and lint (Layer 2).\n3. Build real runtime/formal verification so agents prove behavior (Layer 3).\n4. **Only then** scale parallelism and outer-loop automation (Layers 4–5).\n\n---\n\n**Next Article:** [02. Codebase as a Garden & Local-Context Architecture](./02-codebase-as-a-garden.md) →\n"},{"id":"02-codebase-as-a-garden.md","title":"02. Codebase as a Garden & Local-Context Architecture","md":"# 02. Codebase as a Garden & Local-Context Architecture\n\n> **The Axiom:** *\"Assume every contributor is an agent that sees only the files it opened, copies the nearest example, and takes the shortest path that compiles. Design the repo so a change that looks right from one file is right for the whole repo.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Your Codebase Is Your Largest Prompt\n\nDevelopers obsess over crafting the perfect 500-line system prompt while ignoring a far larger input sitting right in front of the model: **the repository itself**.\n\nWhen an agent opens three files to implement a feature, 95% of its context window is your existing code. If your repository contains:\n- Two competing ways to fetch data,\n- A deprecated state store kept around \"just in case,\"\n- A hacky `setTimeout` workaround with a polite comment explaining why it was necessary,\n- Or an 8,000-line \"god file\" where everything gets appended,\n\nthen **every new agent assumes those patterns are the house style and faithfully reproduces them**.\n\nLauren Tan compares an agent-driven codebase to a **garden**:\n- Code no longer arrives a few carefully pruned branches a week; it arrives like fast-growing organic vines.\n- Your architecture, directory structure, and static guardrails are the **concrete trellis** that new growth climbs around.\n- Leftover workarounds and deprecated APIs are **weeds**. In a human-only repo, weeds sit quietly in a corner. In an agent-assisted repo, **weeds pollinate every new PR at machine speed**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Two Real-World Case Studies From Lauren Tan's Work\n\n### Case Study A: From Eight 10,000-Line God Files to Dune\n\nEarly versions of Grok Bot consisted of roughly **eight massive files exceeding 10,000 lines each**. Whenever an agent was asked to add a feature, the path of least resistance was obvious: append another block of logic to the nearest giant file. Prompting agents to \"please modularize\" failed repeatedly because without explicit conventions, every task forced the agent to invent an architecture from scratch.\n\nHer team's fix was **Dune**—an internal desktop framework she describes as *\"Next.js for Electron apps.\"* Dune wasn't technically exotic; its superpower was **making structure unavoidable**:\n\n1. **Feature-per-directory with standardized discovery:** Every feature lives in its own folder and hooks into the app through one blessed registration mechanism. An agent never wonders *\"where does this new feature go?\"*\n2. **Context encoded directly in top-level directory names:** In Electron apps (including Cursor), agents constantly imported main-process Node code into the UI renderer thread, stalling the UI with long tasks. Dune eliminated the ambiguity with three explicit top-level directories:\n   ```text\n   electron-renderer/   # UI only (features/, client/, primitives/)\n   electron-main/       # Main process only (startup/, notifications/, update/)\n   shared/              # Pure types & contracts shared by both sides\n   ```\n   A dependency-graph analyzer forbids imports across `electron-main/` and `electron-renderer/`—**not even type imports**. The directory name teaches the architecture; static analysis enforces it.\n3. **Banning nuanced footguns locally:** React's `useEffect` has legitimate uses, *\"but people joke you need a PhD to use it properly\"*—and agents misuse it constantly (infinite render loops, stale closures, race conditions). Dune banned `useEffect` outright in ordinary product code, allowing it only inside narrow, audited framework primitives. An entire category of PR bugs vanished permanently.\n\n### Case Study B: How One Workaround Became 183 Timers in Five Weeks\n\nAt Cursor, an underlying architectural clash existed between VS Code's imperative style and React's declarative style. Look at how a single tiny compromise mutated into a product-wide performance crisis:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    T1[\"Sep 9<br/>Agent patches a UI flicker with setTimeout()<br/>+ comment: 'proper fix is a larger change'\"] --> T2[\"Oct 3<br/>Next agent sees the commented pattern<br/>and copies it into a shared utility module\"]\n    T2 --> T3[\"Oct 17<br/>183 imperative timers across the codebase<br/>→ rapid allocation/GC pauses → visible UI jank\"]\n```\n\nNotice the lesson: **an architectural mismatch doesn't just make the runtime slower—it makes every agent worse**, because agents reach for timers and hacks to paper over the clash, and then copy each other's hacks.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Five Architectural Principles That Keep the Garden Clean\n\nDrawn directly from `pstack`'s core & architecture principles, these five habits turn a repository into an environment where agents naturally produce clean code.\n\n### 3.1 Subtract Before You Add & Delete Legacy APIs in One Wave\n*Principles: `Subtract Before You Add` + `Migrate Callers Then Delete Legacy APIs` + `Outcome-Oriented Execution`*\n\nWhen introducing a new pattern or refactoring an old one, human teams often keep the old helper around for \"gradual migration.\" In an agent-assisted codebase—especially a **solo** one with no external backward-compatibility promises—**coexisting old and new APIs are poison**.\n\n- **Rule:** Sequence **removal before construction**. Delete dead code, obsolete adapters, and speculative validators *first*, then build on the smaller base.\n- **Rule:** Migrate all callers and delete the old internal API in the **same wave/PR**. Leave zero deprecated examples for the next agent to imitate.\n- **Rule (`Outcome-Oriented Execution`):** During planned rewrites with clear phase boundaries, converge straight on the target architecture rather than writing throwaway compatibility shims that harden into permanent debt.\n\n**Example (Backend Repository Migration):**\n```ts\n// BAD: Leaving v1 alongside v2 so agents randomly copy v1 next week\nexport function getUserLegacy(id: string) { /* ... */ } // @deprecated use fetchUser\nexport function fetchUser(ctx: TenantContext, id: UserId) { /* ... */ }\n\n// GOOD: Migrate every caller in one sweep, delete getUserLegacy, and let the compiler verify 0 callers remain.\n```\n\n### 3.2 One Owner, One Way\n*Principles: `Foundational Thinking` + `Laziness Protocol` + `/architect`*\n\nBefore writing code that crosses a function or module boundary, sketch **two competing shapes** and pick the one where:\n1. **Each piece of state has exactly one owner.** Never maintain two hand-synced lists, two caches, or mirrored config files.\n2. **Each task has exactly one supported way.** One HTTP client, one logger, one error format, one way to register a CLI subcommand.\n3. **\"DRY the structure, not every line.\"** Consolidating decisions behind one source of truth beats abstracting three similar lines into a generic helper full of boolean flags.\n\n### 3.3 Minimize Reader Load (The 30-Second Agent Test)\n*Principle: `Minimize Reader Load`*\n\nReader load is the mental effort required to trace code—and **it hurts LLM reasoning just as much as human comprehension**. It has two independent axes:\n\n1. **Layers of indirection** between a question and its answer.\n2. **Hidden or mutable state** that must be held in working memory.\n\n| High Reader Load (Agent Confusion) | Low Reader Load (Agent Clarity) |\n|---|---|\n| `Route → Controller → Service → Manager → Repository → DB` (5 pass-through layers with 1 caller each) | `Route (parse + auth) → Domain pure fn → Repository (query)` — collapse one-caller wrappers! |\n| Shared module-level mutable cache mutated by 4 helpers | Return new values; prefer `returns > locals > fields > module state > globals`. Derive instead of sync. |\n| Re-checking `if (!user)` in 6 internal helpers | Validate once at the boundary; trust the internal type everywhere else. |\n\n> **The 30-Second Test:** Open any file cold. Can a new reader (or an agent with 1–2 files open) answer **\"Where does `X` come from?\"** and **\"What can change `X`?\"** in under 30 seconds? If not, collapse layers or shrink mutable scope.\n\n### 3.4 Redesign From First Principles (Refusing Bolt-On Flags)\n*Principle: `Redesign From First Principles`*\n\nWhen a new requirement arrives, an agent's default move is to bolt an optional parameter or `if (mode === \"special\")` branch onto an existing function. Three features later, the function is an unmaintainable tangle.\n\nInstead, pause and ask: **\"If we were designing this module from scratch knowing this requirement existed on day one, what would the data shape look like?\"**\n\n```ts\n// BOLTED ON: 3 features added as optional flags -> 8 possible combinations, half invalid\nfunction exportData(rows: Row[], asJson?: boolean, pretty?: boolean, includeHeaders?: boolean) { ... }\n\n// REDESIGNED FROM FIRST PRINCIPLES: Explicit format union -> every state is valid & self-documenting\ntype ExportFormat =\n  | { kind: \"csv\"; includeHeaders: boolean }\n  | { kind: \"json\"; pretty: boolean };\n\nfunction exportData(rows: Row[], format: ExportFormat): string { ... }\n```\n\n### 3.5 Ban Your Local Footguns (The Dune Pattern for Solo Repos)\n\nYou don't need to copy SpaceXSI's exact Electron bans—you need to copy **the method**. Every stack has 2–4 nuanced APIs that agents repeatedly misuse:\n\n| Surface | Nuanced Footguns Agents Misuse | Narrow Blessed Primitive to Provide Instead |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Web / React** | Raw `useEffect` for data fetching/sync; scattered `setTimeout` / `requestAnimationFrame` | Dedicated query hooks (`useQuery`), declarative transitions, or one audited timer hook |\n| **Backend / API** | Raw SQL string interpolation; direct DB client imports in HTTP handlers; un-timed `fetch()` | Typed repository module requiring `TenantContext`; shared `httpClient` with timeout + retry budget |\n| **CLI** | Scattered `process.exit()` and `console.log()` deep in logic; non-atomic `fs.writeFileSync` | Pure command functions returning structured `Result`; single renderer for text/`--format json`; atomic `writeFileAtomic()` helper |\n\nPut the safe primitive in one file, delete every raw call site, and enforce the ban at Layer 2 so the footgun can never return.\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [01. The Inverse Pyramid of Trust](./01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md) | **Next:** [03. Types, Boundaries & Domain Modeling](./03-types-boundaries-and-domain-modeling.md) →\n"},{"id":"03-types-boundaries-and-domain-modeling.md","title":"03. Types, Boundaries & Domain Modeling","md":"# 03. Types, Boundaries & Domain Modeling\n\n> **Core Philosophy:** *\"Encode the domain in a data structure instead of scattering it across conditionals. The right structure makes invalid states unrepresentable and deletes branches.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Type Systems Matter 10× More With AI Agents\n\nA strict type system used to be viewed mainly as documentation and refactoring safety for human teams. With AI agents, a strong type system becomes something far more powerful: **a real-time formal constraint solver that narrows the space of programs the agent is allowed to generate.**\n\nWhen types are loose (`string`, `any`, optional-property bags), an agent can write plausible-looking code that compiles cleanly and fails subtly at runtime. When types are tight, every mistake produces an immediate compiler diagnostic in milliseconds—before a single test or human review runs.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Model the Domain: Data Structures Delete Branches\n\n*Principle: `Model the Domain` + `Foundational Thinking`*\n\nThe unmistakable smell that a module skipped domain modeling is **a feature that adds one more `if/else` branch, or a second boolean flag that must stay in sync with the first.**\n\n### Example 1: Replacing Boolean Bags With a Discriminated State Machine (Web / Async UI)\n\n```ts\n// SLOP: 2^3 = 8 combinations of booleans + optionals.\n// Nothing stops { isLoading: true, data: [...], error: \"Timeout\" } from existing.\ntype FetchState<T> = {\n  isLoading: boolean;\n  data?: T;\n  error?: string;\n  retried?: boolean;\n};\n\n// MODELED DOMAIN: Only the 4 valid states can ever exist.\ntype FetchState<T> =\n  | { kind: \"idle\" }\n  | { kind: \"loading\"; attempt: number }\n  | { kind: \"ready\"; data: T }\n  | { kind: \"failed\"; error: string; retryable: boolean };\n```\n\nNow every consumer uses an **exhaustive `switch`** with a `never` check:\n\n```ts\nfunction renderStatus<T>(state: FetchState<T>): string {\n  switch (state.kind) {\n    case \"idle\":\n      return \"Ready\";\n    case \"loading\":\n      return `Loading (attempt ${state.attempt})...`;\n    case \"ready\":\n      return \"Loaded\";\n    case \"failed\":\n      return state.retryable ? `Retrying: ${state.error}` : `Error: ${state.error}`;\n    default: {\n      const _exhaustive: never = state;\n      return _exhaustive;\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIf an agent later adds `{ kind: \"stale\"; data: T; refreshing: boolean }`, **every single switch statement across the entire codebase fails to compile** until the agent handles `\"stale\"` explicitly. The compiler becomes a repo-wide checklist that cannot be forgotten.\n\n### Example 2: Registries & Tables Over Scattered Conditionals (CLI / Backend)\n\nInstead of repeating `if (cmd === \"sync\") ... else if (cmd === \"prune\") ...` across help text, argument validation, telemetry, and execution—define **one typed registry**:\n\n```ts\nconst COMMANDS = {\n  sync:  { description: \"Sync remote records\", destructive: false, run: runSync },\n  prune: { description: \"Delete stale cache\",  destructive: true,  run: runPrune },\n} as const satisfies Record<string, CommandSpec>;\n\ntype CommandName = keyof typeof COMMANDS; // Derived automatically — zero drift\n```\n\nNotice `satisfies`: it validates every entry against `CommandSpec` *without* widening `COMMANDS` into a generic `Record<string, CommandSpec>`, preserving literal keys for `CommandName`.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Constructive Modeling vs. Defensive Guards\n\n*Principle: `Type System Discipline` + `typescript-best-practices`*\n\nMost developers write a loose type and then scatter runtime `if` checks or comments promising an invariant. **Constructive modeling builds the shape out of parts that are all legal**, so the illegal value cannot even be constructed.\n\n| Invariant Held by Comment or Runtime Guard (Fragile) | Constructive Type (Illegal State Unconstructable) |\n|---|---|\n| `type TimeRange = { start: Date; end: Date }; // start <= end` | `type TimeRange = { start: Date; durationMs: NonNegativeMs };` (derive `rangeEnd()` as a pure helper) |\n| `function pickWinner(xs: string[]) { if (!xs.length) throw ... }` | `type NonEmpty<T> = [T, ...T[]];` → `function pickWinner(xs: NonEmpty<string>)` |\n| `function pairUp(xs: string[]) { if (xs.length % 2) throw ... }` | `type Pairs<T> = [T, T][];` (derive `.flat()` when flat output is needed) |\n| `function transfer(from: string, to: string, amount: number)` | `function transfer(args: { from: AccountId; to: AccountId; cents: PositiveCents })` |\n\n### Branded Semantic Primitives\n\nWhenever two values share the same underlying primitive (`string` or `number`) but mean different things—such as `UserId` vs. `OrgId`, `FilePath` vs. `Url`, or `Milliseconds` vs. `Seconds`—brand them so swapping arguments fails to compile:\n\n```ts\ntype UserId = string & { readonly __brand: \"UserId\" };\ntype OrgId  = string & { readonly __brand: \"OrgId\" };\n\n// A brand is created ONLY by a verifying parser/guard — NEVER via `as UserId` at random call sites.\nfunction parseUserId(input: unknown): UserId {\n  if (typeof input !== \"string\" || !/^usr_[a-z0-9]+$/.test(input)) {\n    throw new Error(`Invalid UserId: ${String(input)}`);\n  }\n  return input as UserId;\n}\n```\n\n### The Anti-Overengineering Safeguard: \"Simplest Total Type\"\n\nA common mistake after learning constructive modeling is over-typing *everything*. `pstack` encodes an explicit counterweight called **Simplest Total Type**:\n\n- Keep plain `T[]` whenever every operation on it is already total (for example, `sum(xs: number[])` returns `0` for `[]`—no guard needed).\n- Strengthen to `NonEmpty<T>` or a branded type **only when the looser type forces a lie at a call site**.\n- **The three tells that a type is too loose:**\n  1. Non-null assertions (`sessions.at(0)!`)\n  2. Unsafe casts (`arr[0] as Session`)\n  3. `\"should never happen\"` throws inside internal code\n\nWhen you see one of those three tells, strengthen the input type (or widen the return type to `T | undefined`) until the lie disappears.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Boundary Discipline: Parse Once, Trust Inside\n\n*Principle: `Boundary Discipline`*\n\nAgents frequently clutter internal business logic with paranoid checks (`if (!user || !user.email) ...`) while simultaneously trusting raw `JSON.parse()` output via `as User` at the HTTP boundary—the exact opposite of sound engineering.\n\n**Boundary Discipline** draws a bright wall between the untrusted outside world and your pure internal core:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    EXT[\"Untrusted External Inputs<br/>CLI flags · env vars · HTTP body<br/>JSON.parse · DB rows · webhooks\"]\n    PARSE[\"Thin Boundary Layer<br/>Validate & parse ONCE via authoritative schema<br/>unknown → Domain Type\"]\n    CORE[\"Pure Domain Core<br/>Trust internal types completely<br/>Zero redundant nil checks · Zero framework imports\"]\n\n    EXT -->|\"unknown\"| PARSE -->|\"Validated Domain Types\"| CORE\n```\n\n### The Two-Question Filter\n\nWhenever you (or an agent) are about to add validation or error handling, ask two questions:\n1. **Is this data crossing a system boundary right now?** If not, the validation is redundant—trust the internal type.\n2. **Can this be a pure function that the shell just calls?** If yes, extract it out of the CLI/HTTP/React wrapper into pure domain logic.\n\n### Schemas Before Hand-Rolled Guards (and Bidirectional Verification)\n\nNever maintain three separate copies of a shape (a schema, a TypeScript interface, and a manual type guard)—they *will* drift apart.\n\n1. **Schema-first (Preferred):** Derive the static type directly from your runtime schema (`z.infer<typeof UserSchema>`, OpenAPI codegen, Protobuf, SQLc, Pydantic).\n2. **Type-first (When an interface already exists):** Annotate the runtime validator with the type it claims to prove:\n   ```ts\n   type User = { id: string; name: string };\n   // If someone adds a field to User or drops one from the schema, THIS FAILS TO COMPILE:\n   const UserSchema: z.ZodType<User> = z.object({ id: z.string(), name: z.string() });\n   ```\n3. **Never write a lying type guard:** A guard like `function isUser(x: unknown): x is User { return typeof x === \"object\" && x !== null && \"id\" in x; }` is **worse than `as`**, because it hides an unverified assumption behind a function name that claims safety.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Correctness Over Time: Idempotency & State Isolation\n\nTwo architectural principles in `pstack` prevent the nastiest class of backend, CLI, and multi-agent bugs—crashes mid-run and concurrent writes.\n\n### 5.1 Make Operations Idempotent\n*Principle: `Make Operations Idempotent`*\n\nCLI commands, setup scripts, database migrations, webhook handlers, and background workers *will* crash halfway through and get retried.\n\n- **Reconcile from actual state, not prior intent:** Read what currently exists on disk/DB, compare it to the desired goal, and apply only the diff.\n- **Make partial artifacts recognizable as stale:** Anything left behind by a crashed run (a lockfile, temp file, half-written record, claimed job) must be detectable as stale on the next run.\n- **Write-then-rename for files:** Always write to `<target>.tmp.<pid>` and atomically `rename()` into place so readers never observe half-written JSON/config files.\n- **The litmus test:** *\"What happens if this runs twice? What if the previous run crashed right before every single I/O line?\"*\n\n### 5.2 Separate Before Serializing Shared State\n*Principle: `Separate Before Serializing Shared State`*\n\nWhen multiple processes, jobs, or parallel AI agents might write to the same file, database row, or git branch, the knee-jerk reaction is *\"let's add a lock / mutex.\"*\n\n`pstack` flips that default: **\"We need a lock\" is a design smell to check first.**\n\n- Ask whether the actors actually share one invariant or are simply publishing independent facts.\n- **Two workers each updating their own key inside one `state.json` is still shared mutation** (race conditions + clobbered writes). Give each worker its own file (`state/<worker-id>.json`), temp data dir (`/tmp/app-verify-$RUN_ID`), or git worktree, and merge only at read time.\n- **Instructions are not concurrency control:** Telling two agents in prose *\"please don't edit the same file\"* fails reliably. Isolate structurally first; serialize with locks/CAS/single-writer queues only when a single shared writer is a genuine domain invariant.\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [02. Codebase as a Garden](./02-codebase-as-a-garden.md) | **Next:** [04. Static Analysis, Agent-Directed Lint & Why Comments Lie](./04-static-analysis-and-anti-comment-hygiene.md) →\n"},{"id":"04-static-analysis-and-anti-comment-hygiene.md","title":"04. Static Analysis, Agent-Directed Lint & Why Comments Lie","md":"# 04. Static Analysis, Agent-Directed Lint & Why Comments Lie\n\n> **Core Philosophy:** *\"Comments go stale, humans stop reading comment slop, and comments give agents an escape hatch to justify skipping the right fix. Worse, comments cannot fail a build. Replace prose warnings with lint rules whose error messages tell the agent how to fix the root cause.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Comments Become Slop Factories in Agent Codebases\n\nOne of the most provocative data points in Lauren Tan's *\"All Code Is Slop (Except Mine)\"* talk was a single bar chart: her team cut code comments in Grok Bot from **65,824** (July 31) down to **1,227**—eliminating roughly **99% of comments**.\n\nThat sounds extreme until you examine **how AI agents actually use comments**. Unlike humans, agents routinely use comments for four destructive habits:\n\n1. **Rationalizing workarounds (\"Permission Slips\"):** When the clean architectural fix touches 6 files and a hacky `setTimeout` touches 1 file, an agent writes the hack and adds a convincing 4-line comment explaining why it was \"necessary for now.\" The next agent reads that prose as authoritative architectural wisdom and copies the hack everywhere (how 1 timer became **183 timers** in five weeks).\n2. **Narrating the obvious:** `// Increment counter`, `// Fetch user from database`, `// --- Helpers ---`. Pure token noise that clutters context windows.\n3. **Unenforceable warnings:** `// IMPORTANT: do not remove this call or sync breaks!` or `// NOTE: keep this list in sync with routes.ts`. Because nothing fails when an agent ignores a comment, these warnings rot silently while giving engineers a false sense of safety.\n4. **Suppressing real bugs:** `// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps` or `// @ts-ignore fine for now`—silencing the very deterministic guardrails meant to protect the codebase.\n\n### The Narrow Keep-List (When a Comment *Is* Legitimate)\n\nIn `pstack`'s adversarial comment reviewer (`comment-sicko`), every comment dies **unless** it qualifies under one of five strict exceptions:\n\n| Exception | Why It Survives |\n|---|---|\n| **Legal / License headers** | External compliance requirement. |\n| **External quirk you cannot reshape** | Non-obvious behavior forced by a third-party vendor, OS, browser, or protocol you don't own (proven true on a live path today—surprises in *your own code* get renamed, typed, or refactored instead!). |\n| **Public API doc contracts** | Structured doc comments defining a public library/SDK contract. |\n| **Issue / RFC links for unexpressible constraints** | A concrete link explaining an external constraint code truly cannot express. |\n| **Faulty/style-only formatter ignore** | e.g., `// prettier-ignore` on an alignment matrix. Correctness or safety lint suppressions (`@ts-ignore`, type/bug rules) are **never** allowed to linger. |\n\n> **The Diagnostic Habit:** Treat long justifying comments (`IMPORTANT`, `do not remove`, `too risky`, `fine for now`) as **scent pointing to a bug or missing static check**. Whenever a comment claims a constraint (*\"must match schema X\"*), ask: *can a type, test, or lint rule enforce this instead?* If yes, encode the check and delete the comment.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Writing Lint Rules *for Agents* (Not Just Humans)\n\nMost lint configs were designed for human developers who already know the codebase. When an AI agent hits a generic lint error, it often thrashes or adds an `eslint-disable` suppression.\n\n**Agent-directed static analysis follows one simple principle:** *every lint error message is a high-priority, un-ignorable prompt injected at the exact moment of mistake.*\n\n### Compare Weak vs. Agent-Directed Lint Messages\n\n```js\n// WEAK: Agent knows WHAT failed, not WHAT to use instead -> may hack around it\n\"no-restricted-imports\": [\"error\", { patterns: [\"**/io/db*\"] }]\n\n// AGENT-DIRECTED: Tells the agent the exact replacement pattern & reference file\n\"no-restricted-imports\": [\"error\", {\n  paths: [{\n    name: \"../io/db\",\n    message:\n      \"Do not import raw db in routes/. Call a typed repository in src/io/repos/ \" +\n      \"(see src/io/repos/users.ts) with a verified TenantContext.\"\n  }]\n}]\n```\n\nThe same principle applies to banning local footguns (like `useEffect`, raw `setTimeout`, or `console.log`):\n\n```js\n// Example: Banning raw setTimeout in UI components (the Dune lesson)\n\"no-restricted-globals\": [\"error\", {\n  name: \"setTimeout\",\n  message:\n    \"Do not use raw setTimeout in web/features/ — imperative timers cause GC churn and race conditions. \" +\n    \"Use useDebouncedValue() or scheduleTask() from web/primitives/timers.ts, or fix the underlying state transition.\"\n}]\n```\n\nWatch what happens inside an agent session:\n1. Agent writes `setTimeout(...)` to patch a flicker.\n2. Agent runs `./scripts/check` (takes 2 seconds).\n3. Linter fails and tells the agent **why** it's banned and **which file** holds the supported alternative.\n4. Agent reads `web/primitives/timers.ts` and fixes the root cause in the very same turn—you never see the bad code in review.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Enforcing Architectural Boundaries Mechanically\n\nDirectory structure only works as a guardrail if cross-boundary imports fail the build—including **type-only imports** when processes/runtimes must stay cleanly separated via `shared/`.\n\n| Ecosystem | Best-in-Class Boundary Tools | Example Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| **TypeScript / JS** | `dependency-cruiser`, `eslint-plugin-boundaries`, Biome / ESLint `no-restricted-imports`, Node `package.json` `exports` | `domain/**` cannot import `io/**`, `cli/**`, or `server/**`; `web/**` cannot import `server/**`. |\n| **Python** | `import-linter`, `tach`, Ruff `TID251` (`banned-api`) | `layers` contract: `cli` / `api` → `domain` → `shared`; `domain` forbidden from importing `sqlalchemy` or `fastapi`. |\n| **Go** | `internal/` package visibility + `golangci-lint` (`depguard`) | `depguard` blocks `database/sql` inside `internal/http/...`. |\n| **Rust** | Cargo workspace crates (`shared`, `domain`, `io`, `cli`, `server`) | `domain/Cargo.toml` literally does not list `sqlx` or `axum` as dependencies—impossible to import! |\n\nNotice how **Rust workspaces** and **Go `internal/`** are Layer 1 perfection: if `domain/Cargo.toml` doesn't depend on the database crate, no agent in the universe can accidentally import it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The Ratchet Pattern: Hardening Messy Legacy Repos Without Stopping Work\n\nOne of the biggest excuses for skipping static analysis is: *\"I already have 50 `any` casts and 12 cross-layer imports in my project—I can't turn on the lint rule without spending a week fixing old code!\"*\n\nLauren's `/correct` workflow solves this cleanly with **ratcheting**:\n> *\"If the pattern is already common, fail only when a change adds more.\"*\n\nHere is a complete, 35-line language-agnostic ratchet script pattern you can drop into any repo (`scripts/check-ratchet.mjs`):\n\n```js\n#!/usr/bin/env node\nimport { execSync } from \"node:child_process\";\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\n\nconst BASELINE_PATH = \"scripts/ratchet-baseline.json\";\nconst baseline = JSON.parse(readFileSync(BASELINE_PATH, \"utf8\"));\n\nconst CHECKS = {\n  // Pattern name -> ripgrep command counting matches in src/\n  ts_ignore_or_any: `rg -n \"@ts-ignore|@ts-expect-error|\\\\bas any\\\\b\" src/ || true`,\n  raw_console_log:  `rg -n \"\\\\bconsole\\\\.log\\\\(\" src/domain src/server || true`,\n  raw_set_timeout:  `rg -n \"\\\\bsetTimeout\\\\(\" src/web/features || true`,\n};\n\nlet failed = false;\nconst nextBaseline = { ...baseline };\n\nfor (const [name, cmd] of Object.entries(CHECKS)) {\n  const lines = execSync(cmd, { encoding: \"utf8\" }).trim().split(\"\\n\").filter(Boolean);\n  const count = lines.length;\n  const max = baseline[name] ?? 0;\n\n  if (count > max) {\n    console.error(`FAIL [${name}]: found ${count}, max allowed is ${max}. Remove the new occurrences:\\n${lines.slice(0, 10).join(\"\\n\")}`);\n    failed = true;\n  } else if (count < max) {\n    console.log(`IMPROVED [${name}]: ${max} -> ${count}. Run with --update to lock in the lower ceiling.`);\n    nextBaseline[name] = count;\n  }\n}\n\nif (process.argv.includes(\"--update\") && !failed) {\n  writeFileSync(BASELINE_PATH, JSON.stringify(nextBaseline, null, 2) + \"\\n\");\n}\nprocess.exit(failed ? 1 : 0);\n```\n\n**Why ratcheting works so well:**\n- Zero upfront refactor tax—you can turn it on in 5 minutes today.\n- **Zero new slop can enter** from this commit forward.\n- Every time an agent cleans up an area, the ceiling drops until it hits `0`, at which point you convert it to a permanent hard lint rule.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Custom Repo-Invariant Scripts & Time-Boxed Exceptions\n\nGeneric linters don't know your repository's domain invariants. Borrow the exact pattern used in this repository's own [`scripts/check-pstack-docs.mjs`](../../scripts/check-pstack-docs.mjs) (which parses English counts, links, slash commands, and model names across docs and fails CI on drift):\n\nWrite small, fast (50–150 line) invariant checks in `scripts/check-invariants.*`:\n\n1. **Expired exception guard:** Require every inline suppression to carry an expiry date (`// lint-exception(expires:2026-12-31): <reason>`) and fail CI automatically once that date passes.\n2. **CLI contract guard:** Verify every command registered in `src/cli/commands/` is documented in `--help` and has a contract test.\n3. **Doc/Feature-map drift guard:** Verify every file path or command referenced in `AGENTS.md` and `docs/verification/` actually exists on disk.\n4. **Prove every new check on a real failure:** Never merge a new static rule until you have run it against an actual past buggy commit or snippet and watched it fail with a clear fix message.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Observe-Only Background Sweepers (Finding 1 Root Cause Instead of 12 Symptoms)\n\nOne of the subtlest gems from Lauren Tan's Matt Pocock interview is her **observe-only background sweeper**:\n\n- She runs a background agent that scans the codebase for React anti-patterns—**but it is strictly forbidden from editing code.**\n- Instead, it appends every finding to a single log document.\n- Every few days, she spends 10 minutes reading the accumulated log and frequently discovers that **a dozen seemingly separate issues are all symptoms of one missing primitive or architectural flaw**.\n\nIf you unleash agents to \"fix every anti-pattern they find\" immediately, they apply 12 piecemeal band-aids. By separating **observation** from **structural remediation**, you fix the architecture once and eliminate all 12 issues at the root.\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [03. Types, Boundaries & Domain Modeling](./03-types-boundaries-and-domain-modeling.md) | **Next:** [05. Verification as the New Code Review](./05-verification-as-code-review.md) →\n"},{"id":"05-verification-as-code-review.md","title":"05. Verification as the New Code Review","md":"# 05. Verification as the New Code Review\n\n> **Core Philosophy:** *\"'It compiles' is not evidence. When agents write code at 100× speed, human line-by-line diff reading stops scaling. The answer is not reading faster—it is spending ~70% of your tokens proving behavior and shifting human review to verification artifacts.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Economics of the \"Golden Age of Verification\"\n\nIn *\"All Code Is Slop (Except Mine)\"*, Lauren Tan cited Cindy Sridharan's observation—confirming it matches her own daily usage: **roughly 70% of daily token spend goes into verifying patches, and only 20–30% into generating them.**\n\nWhy? Because **none of the strongest verification techniques in computer science are new**—runtime end-to-end harnesses, Chrome DevTools profiling, property-based UI fuzzing, TLA+ model checking, and Lean formal proofs have existed for years. Historically, they were simply **too expensive to write and maintain by hand** for ordinary product code.\n\nCoding agents flip those economics completely:\n- Writing and maintaining verification infrastructure is now cheap.\n- Therefore, **you can verify every change far deeper than was ever economical before**—and review **videos, screenshots, traces, benchmark tables, and statecharts** instead of squinting at 800-line diffs.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Defeating Tautological Tests (`Test Behavior, Not Implementation`)\n\nOne of the biggest traps when letting agents write tests is what Matt Pocock and Lauren Tan call **tautological tests**: tests that look green and achieve 95% coverage while proving absolutely nothing.\n\nCommon causes of tautological agent tests:\n- Mocking the exact internal helper doing the work and asserting `expect(mockHelper).toHaveBeenCalledWith(...)`.\n- Computing the expected value inside the test using the same helper/formula as the code under test.\n- Asserting on fake data the test itself constructed rather than state persisted and read back.\n\n### The Two-Edit Litmus Test\n\nFrom `pstack`'s `Test Behavior, Not Implementation` principle, judge every single test against **two imagined edits**:\n\n1. **Edit A — Break the real behavior** (return a wrong value, skip a side effect, write duplicate rows).\n   - *If the test still passes, it is theater—fix or delete it.*\n2. **Edit B — Refactor the internals** while keeping every externally observable output identical.\n   - *If the test fails, it is pinned to implementation details—rewrite it against the public interface.*\n\n```ts\n// TAUTOLOGICAL (fails Edit B, often survives Edit A):\ntest(\"archives project\", async () => {\n  const spy = vi.spyOn(db, \"update\").mockResolvedValue({ count: 1 });\n  await archiveProject(ctx, projectId);\n  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalledWith(\"projects\", { archived: true });\n});\n\n// BEHAVIORAL (catches Edit A, survives Edit B):\ntest(\"archived project disappears from active list and appears in archive\", async () => {\n  const created = await app.post(\"/projects\", { name: \"Alpha\" });\n  await app.post(`/projects/${created.id}/archive`);\n\n  const active   = await app.get(\"/projects?status=active\");\n  const archived = await app.get(\"/projects?status=archived\");\n  expect(active.items.map(p => p.id)).not.toContain(created.id);\n  expect(archived.items.map(p => p.id)).toContain(created.id);\n});\n```\n\nAdditional concrete rules from the principle:\n- **Pair \"this never happens\" with a case where it does happen** (otherwise a no-op passes).\n- **Assert the error type/code callers branch on**, not brittle prose error messages.\n- **Prove a test fails first** on the broken code or a deliberately broken scratch mutation—*a check you have never seen fail proves nothing.*\n\n---\n\n## 3. Giving Agents \"Eyes and Hands\" (Runtime Verification)\n\n*Principle: `Prove It Works` + `Build the Lever`*\n\nMatch the proof directly to what changed—never accept a proxy (like a green build or file timestamp):\n\n| What Changed | Weak Proxy (Reject!) | Real Proof (Require!) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **CLI command** | `tsc` passes / unit test with mocked `fs` | Run the actual built binary in a disposable temp dir; capture literal command, `stdout`, `stderr`, exit code, and `--format json`. |\n| **Backend / Storage** | HTTP `200 OK` from mocked repo | Start real disposable server + seeded DB; call real endpoint; **read the stored value back via a second independent query**. |\n| **Web / App UI** | Component renders in jsdom | Drive the running app via CDP/Playwright using **ARIA roles** (not CSS selectors); capture ARIA snapshot + before/after screenshot/video; reload page to prove persistence. |\n| **Parser / Migration** | Unit test on 2 synthetic strings | Replay a saved real-world input corpus / real snapshot and verify invariants. |\n| **Performance fix** | \"Replaced O(n²) loop with Map\" | Before/after profiles or alternating benchmark runs vetted against a limiter. |\n\n### The Six-Part Verification Contract\n\nLauren Tan calls a project-local verification skill **the single highest-leverage piece of infrastructure in any repo**. Every trustworthy verification harness defines six explicit parts:\n\n1. **Launch:** One deterministic command to start the app in an isolated environment (disposable data dir `APP_DATA_DIR=/tmp/verify-$RUN_ID`, seeded test users/data, known port).\n2. **Doctor:** One read-only health check confirming the exact URL, build SHA, and disposable data dir—so an agent never accidentally drives your personal dev database or a stale process.\n3. **Feature Map (User POV):** A human- and agent-readable \"site map\" of the application from the **user's point of view** (not internal file names). Each feature entry has 4 crisp sections:\n   - *Sub-features* (IDs + 1-line behaviors)\n   - *How to get to it (user POV)* (buttons, keyboard shortcuts, CLI commands)\n   - *Driving recipes* (exact literal commands + observable assertions)\n   - *Gotchas* (traps that invalidate runs, e.g., *\"pressing `n` inside an input types 'n' instead of opening a new note\"*)\n4. **Evidence:** Explicit rules for what proof to save (screenshots, ARIA snapshots, CLI output, read-back queries) and where to put it.\n5. **Cleanup:** Kill only the processes started by this run and clean up fixtures—**without deleting the proof artifacts**.\n6. **Helpers (The Control Lever):** A reusable control CLI so agents don't waste tokens writing throwaway Playwright/curl scripts from scratch every session.\n\n### The Mechanical vs. Judgment Split (`Build the Lever`)\n\nLauren noticed that when she told agents *\"verify this in the browser\"*, each agent wrote its own fragile throwaway script, spent half its tokens debugging the script, and threw it away.\n\nHer fix is a universal engineering rule: **move every repeatable mechanical step into a tiny, tested CLI; save the LLM strictly for judgment.**\n\nSix traits of an agent-friendly control CLI (applicable to both your verification tools and your own CLI products):\n1. **Few composable commands** doing real work (e.g., `verify-ctl doctor`, `verify-ctl browser click --role button --name \"Save\"`, `verify-ctl cli -- ...`).\n2. **`--dry-run`** on anything destructive.\n3. **Progressive subcommands** that don't dump 80 flags at once.\n4. **Actionable error messages** that tell the agent what to run instead.\n5. **Rich `--help`** text.\n6. **Machine-readable output (`--format json`)** so agents never parse fragile human tables with regex.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Scientific Rigor for Numbers (`Explain the Number`)\n\nBefore trusting or shipping *any* measured number—a speedup, latency drop, memory savings, or eval score—apply `pstack`'s 7-question scientific audit:\n\n1. **Write the decision threshold before you run:** What result would actually change your decision? What is the noise floor? (Stop if run-to-run spread exceeds the claimed effect.)\n2. **Ask \"Why isn't it double?\"** Identify the actual physical or code-path **limiter** from a real profile or hardware/runtime counters—a guess from reading source code does not count.\n3. **Verify production tuning:** Did *both* baseline and treatment run with release flags, realistic data sizes, and identical builds?\n4. **Sanity-check physical limits:** Does the claimed throughput accidentally exceed disk bandwidth, network latency, or core count (a telltale sign that work was cached or skipped)?\n5. **Check for silent errors or wrong output:** Fast wrong answers don't count.\n6. **Run alternating trials:** Report run count, **median**, and **spread/range** across alternating baseline/treatment runs (eliminating thermal throttling or warm-cache bias).\n7. **Confirm end-to-end relevance & timed-region honesty:** Did the real work actually execute *inside* the timed region (not lazily deferred or memoized), and does it move a path users actually wait on?\n\nIf you cannot name the limiter or rule out skipped work, the verdict is **`inconclusive`**—never `\"faster.\"`\n\n---\n\n## 5. Formal Verification & Visual Statecharts: Proving What Tests Never Reach\n\nRuntime tests prove the paths you think to drive. The nastiest bugs in backend, sync, and UI code live on **paths nobody drives**: two retries overlapping, an auth token expiring mid-request, an offline edit colliding with a stale sync, or a list virtualizer oscillating on a rare scroll delta.\n\nLauren Tan's team layers three formal/property tools on top of runtime verification—not as silver bullets, and not for ordinary CRUD, but for **high-consequence invariants**:\n\n| Technique | Tool Used by Grok Bot | What It Excels At |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Model Checking** | **TLA+ / TLC** | Exhaustively exploring every possible interleaving of concurrent/multi-step user flows (e.g., notifications across multiple devices, job leases, offline/online sync). |\n| **Formal Proofs** | **Lean** | Proving algorithmic invariants hold for *all* inputs/sizes (e.g., their custom list virtualization math never flickers or jitters). |\n| **Property-Based UI Fuzzing** | **Bombadil** | Hammering the real UI with generated interaction sequences to verify global invariants (no crashes, no stuck loading spinners, no lost edits). |\n\n### Turning Formal Specs Into Human-Readable Statecharts\n\nThe traditional problem with TLA+ is that product owners, designers, and even busy engineers don't want to read raw temporal logic. Lauren's solution was building a compiler (`statechart`) that runs the TLC model checker and compiles the state space into a **Mermaid statechart labeled in plain user terms**:\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> OnlineSynced: App opens\n    OnlineSynced --> OfflineEditing: Connection drops & user edits\n    OfflineEditing --> Syncing: Connection returns\n    Syncing --> OnlineSynced: Server acks\n    OfflineEditing --> BugShownSynced: Stale toast fires [INVARIANT BROKEN!]\n```\n\n- **Why this changes code review:** A screenshot proves *one* happy state looked fine. A model-checked statechart shows **every reachable user-visible state**, highlights any state that breaks a user rule (*\"you must never see 'Synced' while offline\"*), and gives the **shortest step-by-step path** to trigger the bug—which the agent then replays against the real running code to confirm it isn't just a modeling error.\n- **Keep formal checks honest:** Record the exact commit SHA each formal model was last synced to, and whenever covered code changes, re-sync and distinguish **model drift** from a **real product bug**.\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [04. Static Analysis & Why Comments Lie](./04-static-analysis-and-anti-comment-hygiene.md) | **Next:** [06. Debugging, Design & Scientific Engineering](./06-debugging-design-and-scientific-thinking.md) →\n"},{"id":"06-debugging-design-and-scientific-thinking.md","title":"06. Debugging, Design & Scientific Engineering","md":"# 06. Debugging, Design & Scientific Engineering\n\n> **Core Philosophy:** *\"Fix the fault, not the place where it surfaced. When two or more fixes sharing one premise fail the same gate, stop fixing—test the premise. And when design choices are uncertain, remember: the best spec is running code.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Fix Root Causes, Never Symptom Guards\n\n*Principle: `Fix Root Causes`*\n\nAI agents—and hurried humans—have a dangerous reflex when debugging: **patch the line that threw the error.** If `user.profile.email` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined`, the shortest patch is `user?.profile?.email ?? \"\"`.\n\nThat single line of defensive code is **the seed of architectural rot**.\n\n### The Legitimacy Question\n\nBefore adding *any* guard, fallback, retry, clamp, or `try/catch`, ask one razor-sharp question:\n\n> **\"Is the state that triggered this symptom legitimate?\"**\n\n- **If YES** (e.g., an optional profile field that real users may leave blank): handle it explicitly in the domain model and type signature.\n- **If NO** (e.g., an authenticated route somehow received a `user` without a `profile`, even though signup always creates one): **a null guard hides the bug**. Trace backwards until you find the *first* place the system violated the invariant (perhaps a broken migration, a partial write, or a missing transaction) and fix it there.\n\n```ts\n// SYMPTOM GUARD (hides corruption and silently sends blank emails):\nfunction sendReceipt(order: Order) {\n  if (!order.customer?.email) return; // \"fixed\" the crash!\n  mailer.send(order.customer.email, renderReceipt(order));\n}\n\n// ROOT-CAUSE FIX:\n// 1. Trace why an Order existed without a customer email (guest checkout skipped validation).\n// 2. Enforce email in OrderSchema at the checkout boundary + DB NOT NULL constraint.\n// 3. Fix every other checkout entry point with the same fault (fix the pattern, not the instance).\n```\n\nFive classic ways agents accidentally hide root causes:\n1. Defensive null guards (`?.` / `?? \"\"`) for impossible states\n2. Blind retries (`for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++)`) papering over race conditions\n3. Swallowed exceptions (`catch { return null; }`)\n4. Clamping out-of-range numbers (`Math.max(0, balance)`) instead of fixing why balance went negative\n5. `setTimeout` delays masking un-synchronized state transitions\n\n**Bonus habit:** If finding the root cause required a 30-minute deep dig, ask: *what cheap, always-on signal (assertion, structured log field, or invariant check) would have exposed this in 10 seconds?* Add that signal before moving on.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Attack the Premise: Escaping Retry Loops\n\n*Principle: `Attack the Premise`*\n\nEvery developer who works with coding agents has watched the **\"Death Spiral\"**:\n- Attempt 1 fails CI → agent tweaks a regex → Attempt 2 fails the same test → agent adds a sleep → Attempt 3 fails again → agent tries a third workaround...\n\nWhy does this happen? Because all three fixes quietly share **one false hidden assumption** (e.g., *\"the cache is stale\"*, *\"the server hasn't started yet\"*, *\"the parser is misreading UTF-8\"*).\n\n`pstack` turns escaping the death spiral into a hard rule:\n\n> **When two or more fixes that share one premise fail the same gate, STOP writing fixes. Write the premise down as a single sentence, and test the premise directly.**\n\n### Worked Example (Backend / CLI Debugging)\n\nSuppose a CLI export intermittently produces truncated JSON on CI.\n- **Fix 1 (Failed):** Increased flush timeout before exit.\n- **Fix 2 (Failed):** Switched from `console.log` to `process.stdout.write` with callback.\n- **Trigger (`Attack the Premise`):** Stop! Both fixes assumed: *\"Our CLI process is exiting before stdout finishes flushing.\"*\n- **Direct Discriminating Test:** Log the exact byte count written by the CLI to a temp file *and* compare it against what the parent test harness read from `execSync`.\n- **Observation:** The file has 1.4 MB (complete); `execSync` truncated at exactly `1,048,576` bytes—Node's default `maxBuffer`!\n- **Lesson:** Look at **how failures are distributed** (exact 1 MiB cliff!), not just how often they happen. Testing the premise took 60 seconds and saved an hour of blind flailing.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Prototypes Over Abstract Plans (\"The Best Spec Is Code\")\n\n*Principles: `Exhaust the Design Space` + `Experience First` + `Foundational Thinking`*\n\nOne of Lauren Tan's strongest engineering opinions—explicitly called out in `pstack`'s README under *\"Why are there no planning skills?\"*—is:\n\n> **\"Personally, I don't believe in abstract planning. The best spec is code.\"**\n\nWhy? Because **adversarial review of an abstract Markdown plan invents hypothetical risks that never happen while missing the real constraints you only discover when code touches reality.**\n\n### When to Prototype Instead of Debating in Prose\n\n1. **Empirical forks:** *\"Will a virtualized list handle 50k rows smoothly without layout thrashing?\"* Don't debate in 4 paragraphs—spend 3 minutes building a throwaway benchmark/prototype and measure it.\n2. **Novel UX / feel decisions (`Exhaust the Design Space`):** *\"Which layout feels better for the diff viewer?\"* Build **2–3 competing throwaway HTML prototypes** behind a switcher, click through them side by side, pick the winner from lived feel, and throw the prototypes away.\n3. **Contested architectures:** Spawn 2–3 competing sketches on the *same* small brief, compare how clean the resulting types and call sites actually look, pick the best base, and graft the strongest ideas from the others.\n\n### Experience First vs. Foundational Thinking (Target vs. Sequence)\n\nTwo principles work as a pair so you never build beautiful architecture for the wrong experience—or a slick UI on rotting foundations:\n\n- **`Experience First` governs the TARGET:** Who is the user? For a web app, the end user; for a CLI, the developer typing commands; for an internal library, the engineer (or agent) importing it next month. Ship 3 polished, delightful capabilities over 10 rough ones.\n- **`Foundational Thinking` governs the SEQUENCE:** Once you know the target experience, remove dead code first, nail the core data structures and shared types next, scaffold CI/verification early, and *then* build features so every downstream line becomes obvious.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Sequence Work Into Verifiable Units\n\n*Principle: `Sequence Work into Verifiable Units`*\n\nLarge, sprawling diffs are where AI agents lose coherence and humans lose the will to review. Whether you are doing a 20-file migration, a multi-surface feature (DB schema → API → CLI → Web UI), or a refactor, structure every step as a **verifiable bracket**:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    G1[\"Known-Good State\"] --> C1[\"One Small Atomic Change\"]\n    C1 --> V1[\"Run Check (Static + Runtime)\"]\n    V1 -- \"Green\" --> K1[\"Commit Unit\"] --> G2[\"Next Unit\"]\n    V1 -- \"Red\" --> F1[\"Fix or Discard Immediately\"] --> C1\n```\n\n### Order Delivery So the History Proves Itself\n\nEven as a solo developer, ordering commits cleanly makes `git log`, `git bisect`, and morning audits effortless:\n\n1. **Bug fix:** Commit 1 adds the failing behavioral test (or repro script) → Commit 2 adds the minimal root-cause fix turning it green.\n2. **Refactor + Feature:** Commit 1 deletes dead code (*Subtract Before You Add*) → Commit 2 reshapes structure with behavior unchanged → Commit 3 adds the new feature cleanly on top.\n3. **Performance work:** Commit 1 adds the repeatable benchmark harness & baseline measurement → Commit 2+ lands **one commit per verified speedup**.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Scientific Hillclimbing (Optimizing Metrics Without Guessing)\n\nWhen optimizing latency, memory, startup time, bundle size, or accuracy, prompting an agent *\"make this faster\"* usually produces messy micro-optimizations that add complexity without moving the needle.\n\nInstead, run a **scientific hillclimbing loop**:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    B[\"1. Establish vetted baseline & identify the real limiter (profile / trace)\"]\n    H[\"2. Form ONE concrete hypothesis targeting that limiter\"]\n    E[\"3. Make the smallest change implementing that hypothesis\"]\n    M[\"4. Measure with alternating runs (Explain the Number)\"]\n    D{\"Measurable win<br/>above noise floor?\"}\n    KEEP[\"Keep: commit single win & update baseline\"]\n    DROP[\"Discard: git reset & log why hypothesis failed\"]\n\n    B --> H --> E --> M --> D\n    D -- \"Yes\" --> KEEP --> H\n    D -- \"No\" --> DROP --> H\n```\n\nNotice the discipline:\n- **Changes that don't measurably help get discarded**, never left behind \"because they look cleaner.\"\n- **A plateau triggers a pivot** (re-profile to find the *new* bottleneck—or attack the premise), not diminishing-return hacks.\n- **Never relax the finish condition** to declare victory early.\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [05. Verification as the New Code Review](./05-verification-as-code-review.md) | **Next:** [07. From Line Cook to Head Chef: Delegation, Intent & Factories](./07-human-in-the-loop-delegation-and-factories.md) →\n"},{"id":"07-human-in-the-loop-delegation-and-factories.md","title":"07. From Line Cook to Head Chef: Delegation, Intent & Software Factories","md":"# 07. From Line Cook to Head Chef: Delegation, Intent & Software Factories\n\n> **Core Philosophy:** *\"A factory does not rescue a bad process by running it faster. Build a proper Michelin-starred kitchen before opening more restaurants—and once you step back from the stove, remember you are still accountable for every dish that leaves the pass.\"*\n\n---\n\n## 1. The Michelin-Starred Kitchen Metaphor\n\nIn her live interview with Matt Pocock, Lauren Tan offered the cleanest mental model for how a senior software engineer's role evolves in the agent era: **you transition from line cook to head chef.**\n\n- **The Line Cook (Old Model):** Chops every vegetable, cooks every pan, plates every dish personally. Quality is high, but throughput is capped at one pair of hands.\n- **The Head Chef (Agent-Era Model):** Does not cook every single dish on the line—instead, designs the **kitchen layout** (architecture & directory boundaries), builds and sharpens the **prep tools** (CLIs, linters, type systems, verification harnesses), sets the **quality standards** (invariants & taste), hires/coordinates the **brigade** (specialized & fresh agents), and **tastes dishes at the pass** (reviews verification artifacts and spot-checks commits for systemic drift).\n\nCrucially, **stepping back from typing code does not reduce accountability**. When a spot-check reveals a flaw, a Head Chef doesn't just re-plate one dish—they ask: *\"Which station, prep tool, or standard allowed this mistake to leave the kitchen?\"*\n\n---\n\n## 2. Transmission of Intent: Stop Micromanaging, Start Specifying Outcomes\n\nLauren frames the ultimate human bottleneck as **the transmission of intent**—communicating *what you actually want* so clearly that an agent can solve it without constant back-and-forth.\n\n### What Weak Prompts vs. High-Trust Prompts Look Like\n\n| Micromanaged Prompt (Fragile) | Outcome + Evidence Prompt (High Trust) |\n|---|---|\n| *\"Open `export.ts`, add a `try/catch` around line 42, use a `Set` to deduplicate IDs, then update `export.test.ts`.\"* | *\"Goal: exporting twice during a network retry currently writes duplicate rows. Done means: re-running export after a simulated mid-run crash leaves zero duplicates and all existing fixtures pass. Repro the bug first, trace the root cause, fix it, and show me the exact command output proving it.\"* |\n| *\"Work on improving search performance for 3 hours.\"* *(Duration is NEVER a finish condition—you just get 3 hours of motion!)* | *\"Goal: reduce p90 search latency on the 50k-item fixture below 40ms without changing ranked results. Run alternating benchmarks, name the limiter, keep only commits that beat the noise floor, and stop if you hit a dead end.\"* |\n\nFive ingredients of a complete handoff (especially for long or unattended runs):\n1. **Goal:** The user-visible outcome you want.\n2. **Checkable finish condition:** A pass/fail predicate the agent can execute—never a vibe or a timer.\n3. **Required evidence:** What artifact proves success (CLI output, read-back query, screenshot/video, benchmark table).\n4. **Known constraints / boundaries:** What must *not* change (e.g., byte-identical text output, no schema breaking changes).\n5. **Escape hatch:** Permission to stop cleanly and write up the exact blocker if stuck, rather than quietly relaxing the finish condition to fake victory.\n\n> **Pro tip — Hide your hunch at first:** When debugging, don't lead with *\"I think it's a race condition in `cache.ts`.\"* Agents exhibit strong confirmation bias and will search wherever you point. Ask the agent to restate the problem and investigate cold first; share your hunch only after it forms its own evidence-based hypothesis.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Four Laws of Healthy Multi-Agent Delegation\n\nWhen you scale from one chat to parallel or overnight agents, four failure modes immediately appear—and each has a concrete architectural defense:\n\n### Law 1: Author Never Grades Its Own Homework\nThe agent that writes a patch is biased toward believing its patch works (it holds the exact mental rationalization that produced any shortcut).\n- **Defense:** Always separate **author** from **verifier**. Spawn a fresh agent—ideally on a **different model family** (or a second coding harness)—that distrusts the PR description, runs the real app/checks on the exact commit SHA, and keeps only bugs it can prove by running code.\n\n### Law 2: Fresh Context Beats Long Winding Threads (`Guard the Context Window`)\nAs context windows fill up with 40 tool outputs and 3 failed attempts, reasoning degrades, compression loses constraints, and agents start hallucinating or looping.\n- **Defense:** Default to **fresh subagents** for retries, fix rounds, reviews, and bulk file/log scanning. Pass a crisp brief (goal + constraints + prior report/branch pointer) instead of dragging 80k tokens of noisy history forward. Keep the coordinator thread clean and high-signal.\n\n### Law 3: Never Block on Reversible Decisions (`Never Block on the Human`)\nIf an agent stops every 4 minutes to ask *\"Should I name this helper `parseConfig` or `loadConfig`?\"*, asynchronous parallelism collapses.\n- **Defense:** Proceed autonomously on all **reversible (two-way door)** decisions and report what you chose and why. Pause **strictly** for **irreversible (one-way door)** actions: production deploys, deleting non-recoverable data, force-pushing shared branches, or messaging external users.\n\n### Law 4: Audit Decisions, Not Raw Transcripts\nIf an agent works for 2 hours while you step away, you should not have to read 600 lines of chat logs to trust the result.\n- **Defense:** Maintain an append-only **Decision Log (`decisions.tsv`)** recording one row per iteration: `timestamp · phase · decision · reason · evidence_pointer · outcome`. In the morning, read the short *Attention* summary (items requiring human judgment or risk calls) and spot-check the linked evidence artifacts.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Foundations Before Software Factories: The 4 Readiness Gates\n\nLauren Tan's closing warning in *\"All Code Is Slop (Except Mine)\"* is emphatic:\n\n> **\"If your inner coding loop produces slop, automating that loop just produces more slop and burns more tokens.\"**\n\nBefore you ever leave an autonomous loop running overnight—or wire up event-driven bots that open and merge PRs—verify that **all four readiness gates hold**:\n\n1. **You have done the task once by hand (or watched an agent do it live)** so you know what \"good\" actually looks like.\n2. **The agent has the exact same eyes and hands you use:** fast `./scripts/check` static gates, a working runtime verification harness (`verify-ctl` + Feature Map), profiler, and logs.\n3. **Every stage can stop the line:** Triage can reject non-bugs; repro can fail to reproduce and halt; verification can reject a patch and block shipping. Stopping bad work early is a *success*, not a failure.\n4. **You have audited real transcripts** and converted the top recurring mistakes into Layer 1/2 structural fixes (types, boundaries, lint rules, CLIs).\n\n### Prefer Verified Stacks Over Blind Auto-Merge\n\nEven after passing all four gates, Lauren's own progression separates two levels of autonomy:\n- **Level A — Verified Stack (Human Keeps Merge Button):** Agents work in isolated git worktrees, pass static checks, pass independent verifier runs, and produce a clean stack of PRs/branches with proof artifacts attached. You review the evidence in 5 minutes and press merge.\n- **Level B — Full Autonomous Merge:** Only appropriate once weeks of Level A morning audits reveal almost nothing worth reverting, changes are cleanly reversible, and programmatic verification covers the critical invariants.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Putting It All Together: A Solo Multi-Harness Synthesis\n\nAs a solo developer building across **CLI, backend, and web/app** with a mix of tools (**Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, Codex**), Lauren Tan's entire philosophy collapses into one remarkably portable operating system:\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    REPO[\"THE REPOSITORY (100% Portable Across All Harnesses)<br/>• Clean directory boundaries (shared ← domain ← io / cli / server; shared ← web)<br/>• Strict types, schemas, discriminated unions, branded IDs<br/>• Agent-directed lint + ratchet baseline + 5-sec ./scripts/check<br/>• Disposable local launch + verify-ctl CLI + User-POV Feature Maps<br/>• Short canonical AGENTS.md with Rule → Enforcer table\"]\n\n    H1[\"Cursor\"] --> REPO\n    H2[\"Claude Code\"] --> REPO\n    H3[\"Antigravity\"] --> REPO\n    H4[\"Codex\"] --> REPO\n\n    REPO --> LOOP[\"YOUR DAILY SOLO LOOP<br/>1. Author in Harness A (goal + finish condition + proof)<br/>2. ./scripts/check rejects bad shapes in seconds<br/>3. verify-ctl proves real CLI / API / UI behavior<br/>4. Interrogate git diff in fresh Harness B (author ≠ verifier)<br/>5. Repeat mistake twice? Promote it up the Inverse Pyramid!\"]\n```\n\nNotice the elegance of this architecture for a solo builder:\n- **Zero lock-in:** Because 80–90% of your quality lives in Layers 1–3 *inside the repository itself*, switching between Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, and Codex takes zero prompt rewriting—every tool hits the same compiler walls, reads the same Feature Map, and runs the same `./scripts/check` and `verify-ctl`.\n- **Free cross-model diversity:** Using a mix of harnesses naturally gives you independent verification—author a feature in one tool, then open a fresh session in another tool to skeptically interrogate the diff and prove any findings by running code.\n- **Compounding returns:** Every single week, your garden gets cleaner, your static checks get sharper, your `AGENTS.md` gets *shorter* (as prose rules graduate into code and get deleted), and your confidence in delegating larger chunks of work climbs steadily.\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [06. Debugging, Design & Scientific Engineering](./06-debugging-design-and-scientific-thinking.md) | **Next:** [08. Applying the Pyramid to Go (& Other Languages)](./08-golang-and-cross-language-guide.md) →\n"},{"id":"08-golang-and-cross-language-guide.md","title":"08. Applying the Pyramid to Go (& Other Languages)","md":"# 08. Applying the Pyramid to Go (& Other Languages)\n\n> **Core Insight:** *Lauren Tan's talk used Electron and TypeScript as her examples, but the Inverse Pyramid of Trust has nothing to do with JavaScript. In fact, **Go is one of the strongest languages on earth for this philosophy**—its compiler natively enforces architectural boundaries that TypeScript needs extra plugins to approximate, and its static-analysis ecosystem (`golangci-lint`, `go/analysis`, `go vet`, `-race`, native fuzzing, `benchstat`) maps 1-to-1 onto every layer of the Pyramid.*\n\n---\n\n## 1. Clearing Up the Myth: \"There Is No Lint in Go\"\n\nMany Go developers feel confused when they hear talks centered on ESLint and custom lint rules, because Go culture famously avoids formatting wars:\n- **`gofmt`** settled code formatting on day one, so Go never needed Prettier or 200 stylistic ESLint rules.\n- **`go vet`** is intentionally conservative—the Go team only includes checks with near-zero false positives.\n\nThat creates the illusion that Go has \"no lint.\" **The reality is the exact opposite.** Go ships its entire parser, AST, type-checker, and static-analysis framework in the standard library and `x/tools` (`go/parser`, `go/ast`, `go/types`, `go/packages`, `golang.org/x/tools/go/analysis`). On top of that sits **`golangci-lint`**, which aggregates 100+ semantic, architectural, security, and suppression linters into one blazing-fast binary.\n\nHere is the direct 1-to-1 translation from Lauren Tan's TypeScript/Electron stack to Go:\n\n| Concept in Lauren Tan's Talk (TS / Electron) | Direct Go Equivalent |\n|---|---|\n| `dependency-cruiser` / `no-restricted-imports` (`electron-main` vs `electron-renderer`) | **Go `internal/` packages** (compiler-enforced!) + **`golangci-lint` `depguard`** |\n| Banning `useEffect` / `setTimeout` in product code | **`golangci-lint` `forbidigo`** (bans calls with custom fix messages!) |\n| Branded types (`string & { readonly __brand: \"UserId\" }`) | **Go defined types** (`type UserID string` — native & zero-cost!) |\n| Unforgeable validated tokens (`TenantContext`) | **Unexported struct fields** + single constructor in `internal/auth` |\n| Exhaustive discriminated unions (`const _exhaustive: never`) | **Sealed interfaces** + **`exhaustive`** linter |\n| Banning `@ts-ignore` / lazy `eslint-disable` (`comment-sicko`) | **`nolintlint`** (requires named linter + explanation on every `//nolint`) |\n| Dead-code pruning (\"gardening\") | **`deadcode ./...`** (`golang.org/x/tools/cmd/deadcode`) |\n| Runtime profiling & verification (CDP, traces, Bombadil, benchmark-checklist) | **`go test -race`**, **native `go test -fuzz`**, **`httptest` + `t.TempDir()`**, **`pprof`/`trace`**, **`benchstat`** |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Layer 1 in Go: Compiler-Enforced Architecture\n\nGo gives you several Layer 1 superpowers right out of the box—no third-party tools required.\n\n### 2.1 `internal/` Packages = Native Boundary Walls\n\nIn TypeScript, preventing `routes/` from importing low-level internals requires bundler/lint configuration. In Go, **the compiler itself enforces `internal/` visibility**: a package under `a/b/internal/c` can *only* be imported by code rooted at `a/b`.\n\nUse a clean solo layout across CLI, backend, and web-API projects:\n\n```text\nmy-go-app/\n├── cmd/\n│   ├── server/main.go          # Thin HTTP wiring only (wires deps, starts server)\n│   └── cli/main.go             # Thin CLI shell (parses flags, renders text/--format json)\n├── internal/\n│   ├── domain/                 # Pure business types, state machines, rules (ZERO net/http, database/sql, os)\n│   ├── auth/                   # Sole owner of TenantContext construction\n│   ├── repo/                   # Sole owner of database/sql / sqlc queries\n│   ├── httpapi/                # Boundary parsing (JSON -> domain types), status mapping\n│   └── cliapp/                 # CLI command handlers calling domain + repo\n├── pkg/contract/               # Optional shared wire DTOs / OpenAPI-generated types\n├── scripts/check               # Single fast gate run locally & in CI\n└── AGENTS.md                   # Short repo handbook + Rule → Enforcer table\n```\n\n### 2.2 Zero-Cost Semantic Branding & Unforgeable Capabilities\n\nGo doesn't need TypeScript's `__brand` trick. Distinct defined types cannot be accidentally swapped by an agent:\n\n```go\npackage domain\n\ntype UserID string\ntype OrgID  string\n\n// Won't compile if an agent swaps (orgID, userID)!\nfunc AssignOwner(orgID OrgID, userID UserID) error { ... }\n```\n\nAnd to make tenant authorization **impossible to bypass** (the Level 1 example from [Article 01](./01-inverse-pyramid-of-trust.md)), hide fields in an unexported struct inside `internal/auth`:\n\n```go\npackage auth\n\nimport \"my-go-app/internal/domain\"\n\n// Unexported field: NO package outside internal/auth can construct a valid TenantContext!\ntype TenantContext struct {\n    orgID  domain.OrgID\n    userID domain.UserID\n}\n\nfunc (t TenantContext) OrgID() domain.OrgID { return t.orgID }\n\n// Only verified middleware/token parsing can produce a TenantContext.\nfunc VerifyToken(rawToken string) (TenantContext, error) { ... }\n```\n\nNow every function in `internal/repo` accepts `ctx context.Context, tenant auth.TenantContext, ...`. An agent opening `internal/httpapi/orders.go` **cannot even compile** a database call without passing a real `auth.TenantContext` obtained from auth middleware.\n\n### 2.3 Boundary Discipline & Sealed Sum Types in Go\n\n- **Boundary parsing:** Parse `http.Request` JSON, CLI flags, and env vars *once* in `internal/httpapi` or `internal/cliapp` into validated `domain` structs. Inside `internal/domain`, trust the types and write pure functions with zero framework imports.\n- **Sealed sum types (preventing invalid state bags):**\n  ```go\n  type JobState interface{ isJobState() }\n\n  type JobPending   struct{ EnqueuedAt time.Time }\n  type JobRunning   struct{ WorkerID string; StartedAt time.Time }\n  type JobCompleted struct{ FinishedAt time.Time; Rows int }\n  type JobFailed    struct{ ErrCode string; Retryable bool }\n\n  func (JobPending) isJobState()   {}\n  func (JobRunning) isJobState()   {}\n  func (JobCompleted) isJobState() {}\n  func (JobFailed) isJobState()    {}\n  ```\n- **Schema-first codegen:** Use **`sqlc`** (SQL → type-safe Go queries) and **`oapi-codegen`** or **Protobuf** so Go structs are derived directly from authoritative schemas instead of hand-synced copies.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Layer 2 in Go: Static Analysis & Agent-Directed Lint\n\nHere are the three progressive tiers of static analysis in Go—from zero-dependency tests to custom compiler analyzers.\n\n### Tier A: `.golangci.yml` Configured *for* AI Agents\n\nInstall `golangci-lint` and configure it so **every banned import or footgun prints the exact replacement file/function** (the `poteto` principle):\n\n```yaml\nversion: \"2\"\n\nlinters:\n  enable:\n    - depguard        # Cross-layer import walls (Dune's electron-main/renderer rule in Go)\n    - forbidigo       # Ban local footguns (Dune's useEffect/setTimeout ban in Go)\n    - exhaustive      # Require handling every enum constant in switch statements\n    - errcheck        # Never swallow returned errors\n    - errorlint       # Enforce errors.Is / errors.As / %w wrapping\n    - noctx           # Require context.Context on HTTP/SQL calls\n    - bodyclose       # Catch unclosed HTTP response bodies\n    - sqlclosecheck   # Catch unclosed sql.Rows / sql.Stmt\n    - rowserrcheck    # Catch unchecked rows.Err() after DB iteration\n    - nolintlint      # Go's Comment Sicko: force named rule + explanation on every //nolint\n    - staticcheck     # Deep semantic bug detection\n    - govet           # Standard vet + shadow checking\n\n  settings:\n    depguard:\n      rules:\n        domain-is-pure:\n          files: [\"**/internal/domain/**/*.go\"]\n          deny:\n            - pkg: \"database/sql\"\n              desc: \"internal/domain must stay pure. Put DB access in internal/repo/ (see internal/repo/users.go).\"\n            - pkg: \"net/http\"\n              desc: \"internal/domain must stay transport-agnostic. Map HTTP concerns in internal/httpapi/.\"\n        routes-use-repos:\n          files: [\"**/internal/httpapi/**/*.go\"]\n          deny:\n            - pkg: \"database/sql\"\n              desc: \"Do not query database/sql from HTTP handlers. Call a TenantContext-scoped repo in internal/repo/.\"\n\n    forbidigo:\n      forbid:\n        - pattern: '^time\\.Sleep$'\n          msg: \"Do not use time.Sleep in app/test code; use context timers, channels, or a clock interface (internal/clock).\"\n        - pattern: '^fmt\\.Print.*$'\n          msg: \"Do not use fmt.Print* for logging; use structured log/slog via the injected logger.\"\n        - pattern: '^http\\.DefaultClient$'\n          msg: \"http.DefaultClient has no timeout. Use internal/httpclient.New() with explicit timeouts.\"\n\n    nolintlint:\n      require-explanation: true   # Bans bare `//nolint` excuses\n      require-specific: true      # Must name exact linter, e.g. `//nolint:forbidigo // CLI renderer`\n      allow-unused: false         # Fails if a stale //nolint no longer suppresses anything\n```\n\nLook how closely `depguard` + `forbidigo` + `nolintlint` replicate Lauren Tan's entire static-analysis talk—often with *less* boilerplate than ESLint!\n\n### Tier B: Zero-Dependency Architectural Test (`arch_test.go`)\n\nDon't even want to configure an external linter first? Put a 35-line standard Go test in your repo root so plain **`go test ./...`** enforces your dependency graph automatically:\n\n```go\npackage app_test\n\nimport (\n    \"strings\"\n    \"testing\"\n\n    \"golang.org/x/tools/go/packages\"\n)\n\nfunc TestArchitectureBoundaries(t *testing.T) {\n    pkgs, err := packages.Load(&packages.Config{Mode: packages.NeedName | packages.NeedImports}, \"./internal/...\")\n    if err != nil {\n        t.Fatalf(\"load packages: %v\", err)\n    }\n\n    forbidden := map[string][]string{\n        \"/internal/domain\":  {\"database/sql\", \"net/http\", \"/internal/repo\", \"/internal/httpapi\"},\n        \"/internal/httpapi\": {\"database/sql\"},\n    }\n\n    for _, pkg := range pkgs {\n        for layer, banned := range forbidden {\n            if !strings.Contains(pkg.PkgPath, layer) {\n                continue\n            }\n            for imp := range pkg.Imports {\n                for _, rule := range banned {\n                    if imp == rule || strings.Contains(imp, rule) {\n                        t.Errorf(\"ARCH VIOLATION: %s must not import %s (fix: keep %s decoupled; see AGENTS.md)\",\n                            pkg.PkgPath, imp, layer)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nEvery AI harness already runs `go test ./...`. The instant an agent breaks a layer boundary, standard `go test` fails with your custom fix message!\n\n### Tier C: Custom `go/analysis` Analyzers & Dead-Code Gardening\n\n- **Custom type-aware lint rule:** When a rule requires type inspection (e.g., *\"every exported repo method must accept `auth.TenantContext` as its second parameter\"*), write a ~50-line `analysis.Analyzer` using `golang.org/x/tools/go/analysis` and run it directly via `go vet -vettool=./bin/mycheck ./...`.\n- **Automated garden weeding:** Run `go run golang.org/x/tools/cmd/deadcode@latest ./...` periodically to list unreachable functions and delete dead code before agents copy it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Layer 3 in Go: Built-In Verification Superpowers\n\nGo makes Layer 3 (*Prove It Works*, *Explain the Number*, and property-based testing) unusually frictionless:\n\n| Verification Need | Go Native Tool | Why It Shines With Agents |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Concurrency & race bugs** | `go test -race ./...` | Built-in ThreadSanitizer catches shared-state bugs deterministically—zero setup. |\n| **Property-based / edge-case testing** | `go test -fuzz=FuzzParse -fuzztime=30s ./internal/parser` | Native coverage-guided fuzzer discovers panics, round-trip mismatches, and parser crashes tests never wrote. |\n| **Isolated runtime verification** | `t.TempDir()` + `httptest.NewServer` + `testcontainers-go` | Every test gets a disposable temp directory & real HTTP/DB instance—perfect for *Separate Before Serializing Shared State*. |\n| **Statistical benchmarks (*Explain the Number*)** | `go test -bench=. -benchmem -count=10` + `benchstat old.txt new.txt` | Computes medians, variance, and statistical significance (`p < 0.05` vs `~` noise)—prevents fake speedups automatically! |\n| **CPU / heap / goroutine forensics** | `go test -cpuprofile=cpu.out` + `go tool pprof -top cpu.out` | Pure CLI output agents can read directly—eliminates being the \"meat proxy\" for profilers. |\n| **Reusable Control CLI (`Build the Lever`)** | `go build -o bin/verify-ctl ./cmd/verify-ctl` | Compiles in <1s to a single zero-dependency binary with `--format json` and `--dry-run` that works identically in Cursor, Claude Code, Antigravity, and Codex. |\n\n### Your Single Fast Gate (`scripts/check`)\n\nPut one executable script in every Go repo so every harness runs the exact same bar locally and in CI:\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\ngo vet ./...\ngolangci-lint run ./...\ngo test -race -count=1 ./...\n```\n\n---\n\n## 5. Cross-Language Rosetta Stone\n\nEvery principle in this wiki translates cleanly across languages—only the tool names change:\n\n| Pyramid Layer | Go | TypeScript / Node | Python | Rust |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Layer 1: Boundaries** | `internal/` packages + unexported fields | Directory layout + `package.json` `exports` | Package structure + `_internal` | Cargo workspace crates (`pub(crate)`) |\n| **Layer 1: Illegal States** | Defined types (`type UserID string`), sealed interfaces | Discriminated unions, branded types, `NonEmpty<T>` | `NewType`, `Literal` unions, `frozen=True` dataclasses | Enums with data, newtype structs (`struct UserId(String)`) |\n| **Layer 1: Schema Source** | `sqlc`, `oapi-codegen`, Protobuf | `zod` (`z.infer`), OpenAPI, Prisma | `pydantic` v2, SQLAlchemy/Alembic | `serde` + `schemars`, `sqlx` compile-time queries |\n| **Layer 2: Import / Footgun Lint** | `golangci-lint` (`depguard`, `forbidigo`, `nolintlint`) + `arch_test.go` | ESLint (`no-restricted-imports`, `no-restricted-syntax`), `dependency-cruiser`, Biome | `ruff` (`TID251`, `S`, `B`), `import-linter`, `tach`, `pyright --strict` | `clippy` (`#![deny(clippy::unwrap_used)]`), Cargo crate graph |\n| **Layer 3: Fuzz / Property / Bench** | `go test -race`, `go test -fuzz`, `benchstat`, `pprof` | `fast-check`, Playwright/CDP, Bombadil, Vitest | `hypothesis`, `pytest`, Playwright | `proptest`, `cargo-fuzz`, `criterion`, `miri`, Kani |\n\n---\n\n**Previous:** ← [07. From Line Cook to Head Chef](./07-human-in-the-loop-delegation-and-factories.md) | **Home:** [Wiki Index](./README.md)\n"}];
